---
description: Dashboard YAML top-level structure, sections, tabs, and grid layout
globs: dashboards/**/*.yaml
alwaysApply: false
---

<rule>
<meta>
  This rule defines the YAML structure for A7S Skipper dashboards: hierarchy, fields, directory conventions, grid layout, and authoring modes.
  Activate when creating or editing dashboard YAML files in `dashboards/`.
</meta>

<concepts>
  <hierarchy>root → dashboard → tab → section → widget</hierarchy>
  <grid>12-column CSS grid (`grid grid-cols-12 gap-4`). Always 12 columns, always 16px gap.</grid>
  <widget_types>rich_table | markdown | chart — three types only, no others exist</widget_types>
  <notebook_mode>Tabs may use `mode: notebook` to render markdown narrative cells, executable SQL/Python datasource projections, and dependent widgets as an ordered notebook.</notebook_mode>
  <doc_mode>Tabs may use `mode: doc` for Knowledge Base pages, handbooks, onboarding guides, and operating procedures. Doc tabs are normal dashboard tabs stored in root YAML, served at the normal tab URL, and protected by the same tab RBAC resource path.</doc_mode>
  <authoring_modes>
    - **Flat mode**: single YAML file per dashboard
    - **Directory mode**: strict separation — `_tab.yaml` = meta, `_shared/` = data definitions, `NN-*.yaml` = tab content
  </authoring_modes>
</concepts>

<schema>
  <dashboard_top_level>
    ```yaml
    id: my_dashboard           # Required. snake_case, URL-safe
    title: My Dashboard        # Required. Human-readable
    icon: BarChart3            # Optional. Lucide icon name
    description: Description   # Optional. Sidebar subtitle
    appearance:                # Optional
      show_title: true         #   Show dashboard title in header
      show_description: true   #   Show description in header
    datasources: {}            # Required. Named datasources (→ dashboard-datasources rule)
    mappings: {}               # Optional. Value mapping dicts (→ dashboard-mappings rule)
    filters: []                # Optional. Dashboard-level filters (→ dashboard-filters rule)
    freshness: ...             # Optional. Data freshness config (→ dashboard-datasources rule)
    ai: {}                     # Optional. AI assistant config (see below)
    tabs: []                   # Optional. Tabbed layout
    sections: []               # Required. Array of sections with widgets
    ```
  </dashboard_top_level>

  <section>
    ```yaml
    sections:
      - id: overview                      # Required. Unique within dashboard
        title: Overview                   # Optional. Shows header with collapse toggle
        description: Section description  # Optional
        layout: vertical                  # Optional. vertical (default) | horizontal
        columns: 2                        # Optional. For horizontal layout — equal-width columns
        gap: 4                            # Optional. Defined in schema, NOT yet rendered (always 16px)
        collapsed: false                  # Optional. Start collapsed (requires title)
        shareable: true                   # Optional. Share link button (default: true when title set)
        filters: []                       # Optional. Section-level filters
        ai_hint: "..."                    # Optional. AI context (string or string[])
        widgets: []                       # Required. Array of widgets
    ```
  </section>

  <widget_base_fields>
    ```yaml
    - id: my_widget            # Required. Unique within section
      type: rich_table         # Required. rich_table | markdown | chart
      col_span: 12             # Optional. Grid columns (1–12; >12 for wide scroll tables)
      row_span: 1              # Optional. Grid rows (use 2+ for multi-track charts)
      filters:                 # Optional. Control inherited filter scope
        include: [date_range]  #   Only these filters
        exclude: [media_source] #   All except these
      inline_filters: []       # Optional. Widget-scoped filter definitions
      shareable: true          # Optional. Share link button
      ai_hint: "..."           # Optional. AI context (string or string[])
    ```
  </widget_base_fields>

  <tab>
    ```yaml
    tabs:
      - id: overview           # Required. URL-safe slug
        title: Overview        # Required
        icon: BarChart3        # Optional. Lucide icon
        mode: doc              # Optional. default | notebook | doc
        runtime:               # Optional. Notebook runtime selection
          code: pyodide        #   pyodide/browser by default; server runtimes may be configured
        filters: []            # Optional. Tab-level filters
        sections: []           # Optional. Sections within this tab
    ```
    When `tabs` is present, top-level `sections` render above all tabs.
    In notebook mode, cells are ordered from markdown widgets and datasource projections with `code:`. Markdown cells open in edit mode by default; `Shift+Enter` is the primary run-and-advance shortcut for code cells.
    In doc mode, the tab renders as a Knowledge Base reader surface. Use static markdown `content:` for durable prose, or `template:` + inline DuckDB `datasource:` when the prose needs calculated values. Do not create a separate `/docs` route or `kb_pages` store; root storage remains the source of truth.
  </tab>

  <ai_config>
    ```yaml
    ai:
      roles:
        preferred: buyer                    # Primary role key (from _context.yaml)
        extra: [owner, financier]           # Additional roles
      dataset:
        name: Dataset Name
        description: What data is in this dashboard
        language: en                        # Default AI response language (en | ru)
        date_column: date                   # Primary date column
        metrics: {}                         # Named metrics with SQL formulas
        dimensions: []                      # Dimension column names
        profiling: { enabled: true }        # Data profiling
        business: { rules: [] }             # Domain-specific rules
      welcome: "Ask about data"
      welcome_ru: "Спросите о данных"
      examples: ["Total spend?"]
      examples_ru: ["Общие расходы?"]
    ```
  </ai_config>
</schema>

<layout>
  <grid_system>
    The dashboard renders on a **12-column CSS grid** (`grid grid-cols-12 gap-4`).
    Max container width: 2000px. Section gap: 40px. Widget gap: 16px.

    Widget `col_span` maps to Tailwind `col-span-N` classes (1–12).
    Values above 12 fall back to `col-span-12` with horizontal scroll.
    `row_span` uses inline CSS `gridRow: span N`.
  </grid_system>

  <preferred_approach>
    **Use section-level `layout: horizontal` + `columns: N` for multi-column layouts.**
    This is the PREFERRED approach — it produces equal-width columns and keeps widget definitions clean.

    ```yaml
    sections:
      - id: kpi-cards
        layout: horizontal
        columns: 3          # Each widget gets auto col_span = floor(12/3) = 4
        widgets:
          - id: revenue-card
            type: markdown   # No col_span needed
          - id: costs-card
            type: markdown
          - id: roi-card
            type: markdown

      - id: charts-row
        layout: horizontal
        columns: 2          # Each widget gets auto col_span = floor(12/2) = 6
        widgets:
          - id: trend-chart
            type: chart
          - id: breakdown-chart
            type: chart
    ```
  </preferred_approach>

  <col_span_mapping>
    | `columns` value | auto `col_span` | Widgets per row |
    |---|---|---|
    | 1 | 12 | 1 (full width) |
    | 2 | 6 | 2 (half width) |
    | 3 | 4 | 3 (third width) |
    | 4 | 3 | 4 (quarter width) |
    | 6 | 2 | 6 |
  </col_span_mapping>

  <when_to_use_col_span>
    Reserve `col_span` and `row_span` for cases section layout cannot express:

    | Use case | Approach |
    |---|---|
    | Side-by-side widgets (equal width) | `layout: horizontal` + `columns: N` |
    | Side-by-side widgets (unequal width) | `col_span` on individual widgets |
    | Wide table with horizontal scroll | `col_span: 15` (values above 12) |
    | Multi-track charts needing height | `row_span: 2` or higher |
    | Full-width widget (default) | Neither — `col_span: 12` is the default |
  </when_to_use_col_span>

  <antipattern>
    ```yaml
    # BAD — col_span on each widget for equal-width layout
    sections:
      - id: kpi-cards
        widgets:
          - id: revenue-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4        # Unnecessary — use section columns instead
          - id: costs-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4
          - id: roi-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4
    ```
  </antipattern>
</layout>

<options>
  <root_layout>
    `+layout.yaml` — root metadata file:

    ```yaml
    name: Product                           # Required. Root display name
    icon: TrendingUp                        # Optional. Lucide icon
    description: Product dashboards         # Optional
    order: 1                                # Optional. Sort order in sidebar
    layout: tabs                            # Optional. tabs (default) | catalog
    catalog:                                # Optional. Only when layout: catalog
      default_sort: order                   # order | name
      quick_search: true                    # Search card metadata in the catalog landing
      auto_layout:
        other_section: true                 # Unlisted tabs become automatic Other when no wildcard exists
        other_title: Static archive
        other_style: archive_grid          # editorial | grid | dense | spotlight | archive_grid | carousel | index
      show_activity: true                   # Best-effort user/org signal badges
      show_updates: true                    # Repository change badges when available
      sections:
        - id: featured
          title: Featured
          style: spotlight                  # editorial | grid | dense | spotlight | archive_grid | carousel | index
          accent: violet                    # blue | violet | amber | emerald | rose | cyan | teal | indigo
          items: [01-overview, 02-deep-dive]
        - id: archive
          title: Static archive
          style: archive_grid
          accent: amber
          items: "*"                        # One wildcard section gets remaining tabs
        - id: launch-shelf
          title: Recent launches
          style: carousel
          accent: cyan
          items: [10-launch-checklist, 11-field-notes]
    settings:
      default_dashboard: 01-marketing       # Optional. Default dashboard to open
    filter_groups: []                       # Optional. Group tabs with bound filters (→ dashboard-filters rule)
    ```

    Deprecated: do not use `+layout.yaml.links`. Folder links no longer render in the left sidebar; validation only keeps them as a warning for legacy files.
  </root_layout>

  <catalog_layout>
    `layout: catalog` is a folder-level browse mode for dashboards with many tabs.
    It renders existing tab files/directories as cards at `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>`.
    Card clicks keep direct tab URLs: `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>/<tab>`.

    Authoring rules:
    - Use catalog for hubs, static archives, reading lists, onboarding sets, editorial overviews, and 10+ tab dashboards.
    - Static archives include monthly reports, launch notes, retired dashboards, old experiment reviews, and historical references.
    - Keep `tabs` as default for small operational dashboards with predictable daily navigation.
    - `catalog.sections[].items` references direct tab file/directory names, including numeric prefixes.
    - Use `items: "*"` at most once to place all unlisted tabs, or let `auto_layout` create an automatic `Other` section.
    - Section styles: `spotlight` for featured reads, `editorial` for mixed cards, `grid` for regular browse, `dense` for many items, `archive_grid` for static archives, `carousel` for curated shelves. `index` is legacy; prefer `archive_grid`.
    - Add card metadata in the tab file or `_tab.yaml`: `description`, `icon`, `tags`, `owner`, and optional `catalog_card: { accent, badge, label, search_text }`.
    - Use design-system accents only: `blue`, `violet`, `amber`, `emerald`, `rose`, `cyan`, `teal`, `indigo`.
    - Reader mode is not part of catalog MVP; it is tracked separately in spec 108. Data-backed KPI/chart previews are tracked in spec 109.
    - Do not confuse folder-level `layout: catalog` with section-level `layout: horizontal`.
    - Catalog sections may include doc tabs (`mode: doc`) directly; card clicks still navigate to the normal tab URL.
  </catalog_layout>

  <knowledge_base_doc_mode>
    Use `mode: doc` on a tab file or `_tab.yaml` when the content is meant to be read as long-form internal knowledge:

    ```yaml
    id: ops-handbook
    title: Ops Handbook
    mode: doc
    description: Repeatable review rituals and operating notes.
    datasources: {}
    sections:
      - id: body
        widgets:
          - id: article
            type: markdown
            content: |
              ## Operating Rhythm
              Capture durable context here.
    ```

    Rules:
    - Doc tabs require no `runtime`; `runtime` remains notebook-only.
    - Doc content is authored as normal dashboard/tab YAML and works with filesystem, MongoDB-backed roots, and in-memory content maps.
    - Static markdown `content:` feeds a cached, rebuildable DocsArtifact reader: sanitized HTML, plain text, section-aware headings for desktop/mobile navigation, ai_hints, wiki_links, embeds, warnings, and contentHash.
    - The DocsArtifact cache is derived from YAML and remains compatible with filesystem, MongoDB-backed, and in-memory roots. Root storage is always the source of truth.
    - Supported reader syntax includes wiki links `[[target#anchor|label]]`, `<!--ai_hint: ...-->`, callout/details/steps directives, safe embed placeholders, `:kbd[...]`, and `:status[...]{color="..."}`.
    - Prefer short links: `[[metric-glossary]]` displays the target page title automatically. Use `[[metric-glossary|Metrics]]` only when the prose needs a custom label.
    - Link resolution checks exact doc tab path/id first, then unambiguous leaf matches with numeric filename prefixes ignored. Use explicit paths when two pages could share the same leaf.
    - Validation rejects unresolved KB links, ambiguous leaf matches, and missing anchors. Do not leave broken links for the reader UI to explain.
    - The reader side rail exposes `Back to Catalog` for catalog-backed pages and bucketed `On this page` / `Related pages` groups. Related pages show outgoing links first, then backlinks; reader/API responses RBAC-filter every item before exposing titles, hrefs, or snippets.
    - Keep calculated values in DuckDB-backed markdown templates or normal widgets; do not hardcode numbers in durable prose when they can be derived from data.
    - Use meaningful h2/h3 headings; they form reader navigation and future retrieval anchors.
    - Reuse catalog layout for Knowledge Base indexes; do not introduce a second canonical docs collection.
  </knowledge_base_doc_mode>

  <directory_mode>
    ```text
    NN-name/
      _tab.yaml                # Dashboard meta (id, title, description, icon, ai, appearance, freshness)
      _shared/                 # Optional shared definitions
        _datasources.yaml      # Shared datasources (extend)
        _mappings.yaml         # Shared mappings (extend)
        _filters.yaml          # Replaces dashboard-level filters entirely
      00-overview.yaml         # Simple tab (title, sections)
      10-revenue/              # Composite tab directory
        _tab.yaml              # Tab meta (id, title, icon)
        _shared/_filters.yaml  # Tab-level filter override
        00-kpis.yaml           # Tab-content file (must contain sections: [...])
        10-charts.yaml         # Tab-content file (must contain sections: [...])
      20-settings.yaml         # Simple tab
    ```
  </directory_mode>

  <naming_rules>
    | Element | Convention | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Root/tab files | `NN-kebab-case` prefix, step of 10 | `01-marketing`, `10-revenue` |
    | Dashboard ID | `snake_case` | `marketing`, `web5_health` |
    | Widget ID | `kebab-case` | `daily-costs-table`, `revenue-chart` |
    | Datasource keys | `snake_case` | `daily_marketing`, `by_media_source` |
    | Section ID | `snake_case` | `overall`, `by_media_source` |
    | URL segments | `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$` | `revenue`, `cohort-analysis` |
  </naming_rules>

  <merge_hierarchy>
    ```text
    Root _shared/         (base layer)
      ↓
    Dashboard _shared/    (override / extend)
      ↓
    Tab-level             (extend with projections)
    ```

    - **Datasources and mappings**: extend — root is base, dashboard/tab wins on key conflict
    - **Filters**: override — if dashboard defines ANY filters, root filters are completely replaced
    - **Filter groups**: defined in `+layout.yaml` — not inherited from `_shared/` or root
  </merge_hierarchy>

  <shared_definitions>
    A root can have `_shared/` next to `+layout.yaml` with datasources, filters, and mappings inherited by all dashboards in that root.

    ```text
    dashboards/<root>/
      +layout.yaml
      _shared/
        _datasources.yaml    # Inherited by all dashboards
        _filters.yaml        # Inherited unless dashboard defines own filters
        _mappings.yaml       # Inherited by all dashboards
      01-dashboard-a/
      02-dashboard-b.yaml
    ```
  </shared_definitions>
</options>

<examples>
  <flat_dashboard>
    Single file `01-marketing.yaml`:

    ```yaml
    id: marketing
    title: Marketing
    datasources:
      daily_marketing:
        sql: |
          SELECT toDate(install_date) AS date, SUM(costs) AS costs
          FROM marts.marketing_creatives
          WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(install_date)')}
          GROUP BY date
    filters:
      - id: date_range
        type: date_range
        column: install_date
        default: { preset: last_14_days }
    sections:
      - id: overview
        widgets:
          - id: costs-chart
            type: chart
            datasource: daily_marketing
            preset: trend
            series:
              - metric: costs
    ```
  </flat_dashboard>

  <directory_dashboard>
    ```text
    01-marketing/
      _tab.yaml                          # Meta only
      _shared/
        _datasources.yaml                # Shared datasources
        _filters.yaml                    # Shared filters
      01-overview.yaml                   # Tab content
      02-by-source.yaml                  # Tab content
    ```

    `_tab.yaml`:
    ```yaml
    id: marketing
    title: Marketing
    description: Daily marketing performance
    icon: Megaphone
    ai:
      dataset:
        name: Marketing Performance
        description: Daily marketing data with spend and revenue
    ```
  </directory_dashboard>
</examples>

<patterns>
  <global_ai_context>
    `_context.yaml` inside each root provides root-scoped AI behaviour:

    ```yaml
    ai:
      roles:
        buyer:
          title: "User Acquisition Manager"
          focus: ["CPI", "ROAS", "spend efficiency"]
          instructions: "Focus on acquisition costs."
          email: "buyer@company.com"
          slack_url: "https://workspace.slack.com/..."
      style:
        - "Answer like an analyst: direct, specific numbers."
      rules:
        business:
          hint: "Core business definitions"
          items: ["ROAS below 3.0 is considered poor"]
          glossary:
            CPI: "Cost Per Install"
    navigation:
      roots:
        - id: product
          name: Product
          owner: { name: "Jane", team: "Analytics", email: "jane@co.com" }
    ```
  </global_ai_context>

  <product_changelog>
    `_changelog.yaml` in each root — non-technical product updates shown in the Help popover:

    ```yaml
    entries:
      - date: "2026-03-05"
        title: "New KPI cards"
        summary: "Added simplified KPI cards for business users."
    ```
  </product_changelog>

  <ai_hints>
    `ai_hint` is available at 6 levels: datasource, projection, projection column, section, widget, and column.
    Accepts string or string[]. Use for _how to use_ the data (vs. `description` for _what it is_).

    Column `description` acts as `ai_hint` fallback in rich_table columns.
  </ai_hints>

  <canonical_urls>
    - Dashboard: `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>`
    - Tab: `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>/<tab>`
    - Root auto-redirects to default or first tab
    - Hash navigation: `#s:section_id`, `#w:widget_id`
  </canonical_urls>

  <mcp_resources>
    - `skipper://roots` — configured roots list
    - `skipper://roots/<root>/changelog` — product changelog
    - `skipper://query-results/{id}` — ephemeral CSV (1h TTL, lazy-evaluated)
    - `update_root_changelog` MCP tool updates `_changelog.yaml`
  </mcp_resources>
</patterns>

<rules>
  <strict_constraints>
    - In directory mode, `_tab.yaml` must NOT contain `datasources` or `sections`
    - `_dashboard.yaml` is deprecated — use `_tab.yaml` (validator emits warning)
    - Every tab-content file must have top-level `sections: [...]`
    - Standalone section files (`id + widgets` without `sections:`) are not supported
    - Tab directories: `_tab.yaml` = meta; sibling `NN-*.yaml` = content with `sections`
    - Widget types are exactly: `rich_table`, `markdown`, `chart` — no others exist
    - URL segments for root, dashboard, tab must be kebab-case
  </strict_constraints>

  <rbac_notes>
    - Dashboard paths are the canonical RBAC resource namespace
    - Access is granted through org-level grants, not YAML-local declarations
    - Keep root/dashboard/tab IDs stable — renaming changes RBAC resource names
  </rbac_notes>

  <multi_root_input>
    **Singular flags** (direct root): `--root`, `--workspace`, `--board`
    **Plural flags** (parent dir scan): `--roots`, `--workspaces`, `--boards`
    **Env var**: `A7S_ROOTS_DIR` — colon-separated, smart resolution (YAML at top = direct root, else scan children)
  </multi_root_input>

  <design_system>
    - Use named palettes: `default`, `ocean`, `sunset` for charts
    - Prefer design-system tokens over ad-hoc hex colors
    - Keep semantic colors meaningful (success/warning/error/info = status only)
    - If MCP available, inspect `skipper://design-system` and `skipper://design-system/palettes`
  </design_system>
</rules>

